WEBVTT

00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:15.580
<v Ira Glass>I remember back when I was first starting in radio, working at NPR. This was in their old studios on M Street in Washington DC. And my boss back then, and my mentor, Keith Talbot-- who, side note, taught me what was possible with radio-- I would not be here without Keith.
<v Ira Glass>我记得刚开始从事广播工作时，是在NPR（美国国家公共电台）工作。那时是在他们位于华盛顿特区M街的老演播室里。我当时的领导兼导师基思·塔尔博特（Keith Talbot）——顺便提一下，是他教会了我广播的可能性——如果没有基思，我就不会有今天。

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:34.810
<v Ira Glass>Anyway, we were in the studio listening to some recording, and this was back in the days of reel-to-reel tape recorders, and so it was long ago, right? And back then, any reel-to-reel tape that you would throw up on a machine at NPR, it would start with-- I think it was 30 seconds. It might have been a minute of tone. You know what I'm talking about when I say tone, right? It's this sound.
<v Ira Glass>总之，当时我们在演播室里听一些录音，那是开盘式磁带录音机的年代，是很久以前的事了，对吧？那时候，在NPR，任何你放到机器上的开盘磁带，开头都会有——我想是30秒，可能是一分钟的基准音。你知道我说基准音是什么意思吧？就是这种声音。

00:00:34.810 --> 00:00:35.170
<v Ira Glass>[TONE RINGS]
<v Ira Glass>[基准音响起]

00:00:35.170 --> 00:00:41.640
<v Ira Glass>1,000 hertz. This tone actually has a practical function. If you picture-- oh, my god. This is really annoying. Hold on. I'm just going to stop that.
<v Ira Glass>1000赫兹。这个音其实有实际用途。如果你想象一下——天哪，这太烦人了。等等，我把它关掉。

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:42.650
<v Ira Glass>[TONE STOPS]
<v Ira Glass>[基准音停止]

00:00:42.650 --> 00:01:04.360
<v Ira Glass>OK, if you picture a sound meter with a needle that bounces up and down every time there's a sound, the tone is supposed to put the needle perfectly at this one spot on the meter where the black numbers end and the red part of the meter begins. There's a zero at that spot, marking, this is where you want to be. And the tone is just supposed to rest there, rock solid.
<v Ira Glass>好了，如果你想象一个声级计，指针会随着每次声音的出现上下跳动，这个基准音的作用就是让指针完美地停在刻度盘上黑字结束、红区开始的那个点上。那个点有个“零”刻度，标志着这就是你想要的标准位置。而基准音就应该稳稳地停在那儿，一动不动。

00:01:04.360 --> 00:01:08.030
<v Ira Glass>But this particular day with this particular recording, we put it on. And Keith and I--
<v Ira Glass>但是那天，在放这段录音时，我们把带子装上去。我和基思——

00:01:08.030 --> 00:01:08.360
<v Ira Glass>[TONE RINGS]
<v Ira Glass>[基准音响起]

00:01:08.360 --> 00:01:35.840
<v Ira Glass>--watch the meter as the needle first dipped below the zero, then climbed above the zero, and then floated sort of tentatively to the spot that it was supposed to be at the zero and rested there. And Keith said to me, you see that? That's a person in there. That's the hand of the engineer, whoever recorded this, trying to find the right level. That's the ghost in the machine.
<v Ira Glass>——看着指针先是跌到零刻度下方，然后升到零刻度上方，接着又试探性地浮动到应该停住的零刻度位置，并保持在那里。基思对我说，看到了吗？那是里面的人。那是录音师的手，不管是谁录的这个，他正试图找到正确的电平。那就是机器里的幽灵。

00:01:35.840 --> 00:02:02.800
<v Ira Glass>I don't know why that stuck with me, the ghost in the machine. I have been recording people my entire adult life. I started at NPR when I was a teenager. And today, sometimes when I set recording levels, I think about that, the trace that I'm leaving of myself when I push the knob up and down.
<v Ira Glass>我不知道为什么“机器里的幽灵”这句话让我印象如此深刻。我的整个成年生活都在记录人们的声音。我十几岁时就开始在NPR工作了。如今，有时当我设置录音电平时，我还会想起那个词，想起当我拨动旋钮时，我留下了自己的痕迹。

00:02:02.800 --> 00:02:20.770
<v Ira Glass>I think sometimes when you record something, you can't even imagine what it is that you're really capturing or who's going to notice it someday. I was talking to this woman, Michele Dawson Haber, and she told me this story, how she had never known her dad. He died by suicide when she was three months old.
<v Ira Glass>我想有时候当你录下某些东西时，你甚至无法想象你真正捕捉到了什么，或者将来谁会注意到它。我曾与一位叫米歇尔·道森·哈伯（Michele Dawson Haber）的女士交谈，她告诉我一个故事，她从未见过自己的父亲。父亲在她三个月大时自杀身亡。

00:02:20.770 --> 00:02:30.970
<v Ira Glass>And her older sister, Ruth, was five when he died, so she knew their dad. But then as she got older-- this is so sad-- she found that she was not able to remember anything about him anymore.
<v Ira Glass>她的大姐露丝（Ruth）父亲去世时五岁，所以她记得他们的父亲。但随着露丝长大——这真的很令人难过——她发现自己再也记不起关于父亲的任何事情了。

00:02:30.970 --> 00:02:55.240
<v Michele Dawson Haber>My sister spent many, many nights and days crying about not remembering him, and wondering what he was like, and what happened. And so it was her who did all the pining and longing, but I didn't really have any longing of my own, particularly because I had no memories and I was only a baby. And I didn't actually think I had any right to long for him.
<v Michele Dawson Haber>我姐姐花了很多很多夜晚和白天哭泣，因为记不起他，想知道他是个什么样的人，发生了什么。所以是她在怀念和渴望，而我并没有什么特别的渴望，主要是因为我没有任何记忆，而且当时我只是个婴儿。我其实觉得我没资格去渴望他。

00:02:55.240 --> 00:03:00.740
<v Ira Glass>Did you have a feeling as a kid of, like, oh, I should feel more? Or did you just feel like, nah, it's not my thing?
<v Ira Glass>你小时候有没有那种感觉，就是“哦，我应该有更多感受”？还是你只是觉得，不，那不关我的事？

00:03:00.740 --> 00:03:03.670
<v Michele  Dawson  Haber>I think a mix of both.
<v Michele  Dawson  Haber>我觉得两者都有。

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:20.420
<v Ira Glass>Michele's sister Ruth was in a perpetual search for information about their dad, but Michele really had no interest. Her mom had remarried. She had somebody else she thought of as her dad. And then a cousin was cleaning out their uncle's house in Israel. That's where that part of Michele's family is from.
<v Ira Glass>米歇尔的姐姐露丝一直在不停地寻找关于他们父亲的信息，但米歇尔真的没兴趣。她妈妈改嫁了，她有另一个人被当作父亲。后来一个表亲在清理他们在以色列的叔叔的房子时，发现了这个，那是米歇尔家人的故乡。

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:38.810
<v Ira Glass>And the cousin found 25 recordings of Michele's dad singing opera. He trained to be an opera singer, even though his job was connecting people's phones. These were old reel-to-reel recordings. Their dad died in 1965. Michele's sister, Ruth, found a sound engineer who could digitize them.
<v Ira Glass>那位表亲发现了25盘米歇尔父亲唱歌剧的录音。他曾受训成为一名歌剧演员，尽管他的工作是接电话线。这些是古老的开盘磁带。他们的父亲1965年去世。米歇尔的姐姐露丝找了一位录音师来将它们数字化。

00:03:38.810 --> 00:03:50.480
<v Ira Glass>And as she and the engineer went through the tapes, they realized it was a lot of music, but it was also a lot of other stuff besides. Like, her dad would take the recorder to work and record conversations with his customers. He'd record himself composing music.
<v Ira Glass>当她和录音师处理这些磁带时，他们意识到除了大量的音乐，还有很多其他内容。比如，她父亲会带着录音机去上班，录下他和客户的谈话。他会录下自己作曲的过程。

00:03:50.480 --> 00:04:09.070
<v Michele Dawson Haber>And so they were listening, my sister and the sound engineer, to one of these tapes. And she said, we got to call Michele. And so she messaged me on Skype. I was at work. And she said, you got to hear this. And she played me this reel, and it was--
<v Michele Dawson Haber>于是，我姐姐和录音师在听其中一盘带子。她说，我们得给米歇尔打电话。于是她在Skype上给我发了消息。当时我在上班。她说，你一定要听听这个。她给我放了这段录音，是——

00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:10.920
<v Father>[SPEAKING HEBREW]
<v Father>[说希伯来语]

00:04:10.920 --> 00:04:12.300
<v Ruthie>[SPEAKING HEBREW]
<v Ruthie>[说希伯来语]

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:23.750
<v Michele Dawson Haber>--her as a three-year-old, and they were looking at photos. And he was asking her who was in the photo, and it was a little game they played. This is all in Hebrew. He tried desperately to teach her Hebrew.
<v Michele Dawson Haber>——那时她三岁，他们正在看照片。他在问她照片里是谁，这是他们玩的一个小游戏。这些都是用希伯来语说的。他拼命地试图教她希伯来语。

00:04:23.750 --> 00:04:24.660
<v Father>Ruthie, [SPEAKING HEBREW]
<v Father>露丝，[说希伯来语]

00:04:24.660 --> 00:04:29.250
<v Michele  Dawson Haber>"Is that Daddy?"
<v Michele  Dawson Haber>“那是爸爸吗？”

00:04:29.250 --> 00:04:30.890
<v Father>No.
<v Father>不。

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:32.500
<v Ira Glass>"No." I got that part.
<v Ira Glass>“不。”那一小部分我听懂了。

00:04:32.500 --> 00:04:33.640
<v Father>[SPEAKING HEBREW]
<v Father>[说希伯来语]

00:04:33.640 --> 00:04:34.530
<v Ruthie>No!
<v Ruthie>不！

00:04:34.530 --> 00:04:36.020
<v Michele Dawson Haber>"Is it Mommy?"
<v Michele Dawson Haber>“那是妈妈吗？”

00:04:36.020 --> 00:04:36.530
<v Father>No.
<v Father>不。

00:04:36.530 --> 00:04:37.280
<v Ruthie>No!
<v Ruthie>不！

00:04:37.280 --> 00:04:38.460
<v Michele Dawson Haber>"Is it Ruthie?"
<v Michele Dawson Haber>“那是露丝吗？”

00:04:38.460 --> 00:04:39.390
<v Father>[HEBREW] Ruthie?
<v Father>[希伯来语] 露丝？

00:04:39.390 --> 00:04:41.210
<v Ruthie>Yeah, me!
<v Ruthie>对，是我！

00:04:41.210 --> 00:04:42.740
<v Father>Me!
<v Father>是我！

00:04:42.740 --> 00:04:44.380
<v Michele Dawson Haber>"Me!" [LAUGHS]
<v Michele Dawson Haber>“是我！”[笑]

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:45.190
<v Father>Hello, Daddy.
<v Father>你好，爸爸。

00:04:45.190 --> 00:04:46.950
<v Michele Dawson Haber>I felt like--
<v Michele Dawson Haber>我感觉——

00:04:46.950 --> 00:04:48.350
<v Ruthie>[LAUGHS]
<v Ruthie>[笑]

00:04:48.350 --> 00:05:06.600
<v Michele Dawson Haber>--I felt like the floor had dropped out from under me, you know? Everything I thought I understood, it just fell away. I had seen photos of him. I knew a little bit about him, but he wasn't accessible. And this man that I assumed was not accessible to me suddenly became so.
<v Michele Dawson Haber>——我感觉脚下的地板塌陷了，你知道吗？所有我以为我理解的东西，瞬间都消失了。我见过他的照片。我对他了解一点点，但他给我的感觉是很遥远的。而这个我以为很遥远的人，突然间变得如此亲近。

00:05:06.600 --> 00:05:07.570
<v Ruthie>[LAUGHS]
<v Ruthie>[笑]

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:09.950
<v Father>[SPEAKING HEBREW]
<v Father>[说希伯来语]

00:05:09.950 --> 00:05:10.540
<v Ruthie>[LAUGHS]
<v Ruthie>[笑]

00:05:10.540 --> 00:05:11.590
<v Father>[SPEAKING HEBREW]
<v Father>[说希伯来语]

00:05:11.590 --> 00:05:12.930
<v Ruthie>[IMITATING FATHER]
<v Ruthie>[模仿父亲]

00:05:12.930 --> 00:05:24.670
<v Michele Dawson Haber>And it was when I heard him speaking and laughing with my sister-- it was overwhelming. And it was only in that moment that I realized what I had lost.
<v Michele Dawson Haber>当我听到他与我姐姐说话和欢笑时——那种感觉太震撼了。也只有在那一刻，我才意识到我失去了什么。

00:05:24.670 --> 00:05:29.140
<v Ruthie>[HEBREW] Daddy.
<v Ruthie>[希伯来语] 爸爸。

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:33.600
<v Ruthie>[FATHER AND RUTH SPEAKING HEBREW]
<v Ruthie>[父亲和露丝说希伯来语]

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:43.350
<v Michele Dawson Haber>To be honest, in the moment, I felt-- my first emotion was anger because I had never felt the loss of his death. I was too young.
<v Michele Dawson Haber>老实说，在那一刻，我感到——我的第一个情绪是愤怒，因为我从未感受到过失去他的痛苦。我太小了。

00:05:43.350 --> 00:05:47.880
<v Father>[SPEAKING HEBREW]
<v Father>[说希伯来语]

00:05:47.880 --> 00:05:48.790
<v Father>[RUTH LAUGHS]
<v Father>[露丝笑]

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:49.700
<v Father>[SPEAKING HEBREW]
<v Father>[说希伯来语]

00:05:49.700 --> 00:06:07.060
<v Ira Glass>Putting ghosts into a machine-- it just happens when you record anything. You know who understood that very clearly? Thomas Edison, the person who first figured out how to record and playback sound, who created the first phonograph machine back in 1877.
<v Ira Glass>将幽灵装入机器——当你录音时，这就会发生。你知道谁非常清楚这一点吗？托马斯·爱迪生，第一个弄清楚如何录音和回放声音的人，他在1877年发明了第一台留声机。

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:23.660
<v Ira Glass>In an article listing the possible uses for his new invention, one of the things he listed-- preserving the voices of family members so you have them after they die. He says when it comes to this, quote, "The phonograph will unquestionably outrank the photograph."
<v Ira Glass>在一篇列举他这项新发明可能用途的文章中，他列出的一项就是——保存家庭成员的声音，这样在他们去世后你还能拥有这些声音。他说，关于这一点，引用他的原话：“留声机无疑会超过照片。”

00:06:23.660 --> 00:06:42.070
<v Ira Glass>And this idea of using a machine to hold on to the dead, it's also part of a famous bit of audio history, or maybe this is more like a legend. You know that illustration of a dog listening to the old-fashioned phonograph with the caption, "His master's voice"? This was the RCA logo for years and years.
<v Ira Glass>而这种利用机器留住逝者的想法，也是著名音频历史的一部分，或者说这更像是一个传说。你知道那幅狗听着老式留声机的插画，配文是“主人的声音”吗？这是RCA（美国无线电公司）沿用了多年的标志。

00:06:42.070 --> 00:07:08.500
<v Ira Glass>OK, Google "RCA trademark painting." And what'll come up-- you'll see the painting that the logo was based on. You see the dog and photograph are on what looks like a coffin, and here's where there's a question, OK? There's no record of what the artist, Francis Barraud, actually intended here, but look for yourself. Lots of people, me included, we only see a coffin.
<v Ira Glass>好吧，去谷歌搜索“RCA标志油画”。你会看到该标志所基于的那幅画。你看到狗和留声机放在看起来像棺材的东西上，这里有个疑问，好吗？没有关于画家弗朗西斯·巴罗（Francis Barraud）当时到底意指什么的记录，但你自己看看。很多人，包括我在内，都只看到了一个棺材。

00:07:08.500 --> 00:07:27.390
<v Ira Glass>In fact, the dog, whose name was Nipper, first belonged to Francis's brother, and then became Francis's only when the brother died. So in this way of seeing the picture, OK, the master is dead. He's inside that coffin, and the dog is listening to a recording of his dead master's voice.
<v Ira Glass>事实上，那只名叫“尼普”（Nipper）的狗，最初属于弗朗西斯的兄弟，直到兄弟去世后才成为弗朗西斯的狗。所以用这种方式看这幅画的话，主人已经死了。他躺在那个棺材里，而狗正在听他死去的亡主的声音。

00:07:27.390 --> 00:07:44.100
<v Jerry  Fabris>So we're standing in the third floor music room. In the early years, it would have been called the Phonograph Experimental Department.
<v Jerry  Fabris>我们现在站在三楼的音乐室里。在早年间，这里被称为“留声机实验部”。

00:07:44.100 --> 00:08:08.470
<v Ira Glass>I'm at Thomas Edison's lab in West Orange, New Jersey. This is where Edison developed the phonograph. The lab's official name these days is the Thomas Edison National Historical Park. And the curator, the audio curator, Jerry Fabris, showed me around. They've done their best to keep things preserved like they were back in the day, the same piano and recording gear sitting right by Edison's hearing aid, which is basically just a long bell horn that he would hold up to his ear.
<v Ira Glass>我在新泽西州西奥兰治的托马斯·爱迪生实验室。这里是爱迪生开发留声机的地方。该实验室如今的正式名称是“托马斯·爱迪生国家历史公园”。馆长，音频馆长杰里·法布里斯（Jerry Fabris）带我参观。他们尽最大努力保持一切如同往昔，同一架钢琴和录音设备就放在爱迪生的助听器旁边，那本质上就是一个他会举到耳边的大喇叭。

00:08:08.470 --> 00:08:19.540
<v Jerry Fabris>And so they would be inviting musicians up here to make recordings, and also making refinements on the machine to try to improve the sound quality.
<v Jerry Fabris>他们会邀请音乐家到这里来录音，同时对机器进行改进，试图提高音质。

00:08:19.540 --> 00:08:25.770
<v Ira  Glass>So this room-- it's funny, I've spent my whole life in recording studios. This room is one of the very first ones.
<v Ira  Glass>所以这个房间——真有趣，我一生都在录音棚里度过。这个房间是最初的几个录音室之一。

00:08:25.770 --> 00:08:28.410
<v Jerry Fabris>Yeah. One of three, if not the first.
<v Jerry Fabris>是的。三个之一，如果不是第一个的话。

00:08:28.410 --> 00:08:38.800
<v Ira Glass>And it's just like an ordinary room. Like, it's high ceiling and there's normal windows, [CHUCKLES] weeding outside. There's no soundproofing in any way.
<v Ira Glass>它就像一个普通的房间。有高高的天花板，正常的窗户，[笑]外面还有杂草。没有任何形式的隔音措施。

00:08:38.800 --> 00:08:39.460
<v Jerry Fabris>No.
<v Jerry Fabris>没有。

00:08:39.460 --> 00:09:12.020
<v Ira  Glass>Jerry also showed me the machine where, for the first time ever, human beings successfully recorded sound and played it back, Edison's very first phonograph. It's an incredibly simple device-- a brass cylinder, crank, a tube to talk into. Like I said, I've been recording people since I was a kid, really, and it was emotional to see the very first machine that did that. Like, before all the great audio documentarians, before Alan Lomax, before Studs Terkel, everything started right there.
<v Ira  Glass>杰里还向我展示了人类有史以来第一次成功录制声音并回放的机器——爱迪生最早的留声机。这是一个令人难以置信的简单装置——一个黄铜圆筒，摇柄，一个对着说话的管子。就像我说的，我从小就开始记录人们的声音，真的，看到那台实现这一点的第一台机器让人感触良多。在所有伟大的音频纪录片工作者之前，在艾伦·洛马克斯（Alan Lomax）之前，在斯塔兹·特克尔（Studs Terkel）之前，一切都是从那里开始的。

00:09:12.020 --> 00:09:27.140
<v Ira  Glass  On Edison Machine>I'm recording this onto a wax cylinder. I'm sort of over-pronouncing my words and talking sort of loudly because that is the only way to get enough volume into this thing to actually make an audible recording.
<v Ira  Glass  On Edison Machine>我正在把这段录音刻录到蜡筒上。我特意把发音咬得很重，说话声音也稍微大一些，因为这是让这种机器获得足够音量来产生清晰录音的唯一方法。

00:09:27.140 --> 00:09:34.400
<v Ira Glass>Recording on one of those old machines in one of the rooms where Edison worked, it made Edison feel very, very near.
<v Ira Glass>在爱迪生工作过的房间里，用那些老机器录音，让爱迪生感觉非常、非常亲近。

00:09:34.400 --> 00:10:05.050
<v Ira Glass On Edison Machine>Well, from WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Today on our program, we have people who go looking for the ghost in the machine. They turn to mechanical apparatuses of all sorts, looking for those they lost, peering into the static for those they cannot find any other way. Stay with us.
<v Ira Glass On Edison Machine>这里是来自芝加哥WBEZ的《美国生活》（This American Life）。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。今天在我们的节目中，我们要采访那些寻找“机器里的幽灵”的人。他们转向各种机械装置，寻找那些他们失去的人，在静电噪声中搜寻那些他们无法通过其他方式找到的人。请继续收听。

00:10:05.050 --> 00:10:10.910
<v Ira Glass>Act One, "Ghostwriter."
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，“幽灵写手”。

00:10:10.910 --> 00:10:23.090
<v Ira Glass>So when technology's at its best, it helps us by doing exactly what we ask it to do. But it's only this straightforward when we ask for simple things, right? Play this song, send this email.
<v Ira Glass>所以当技术处于最佳状态时，它会完全按照我们的要求去做，从而帮助我们。但只有当我们要求做简单的事情时，它才这么直截了当，对吧？播放这首歌，发送这封邮件。

00:10:23.090 --> 00:10:40.700
<v Ira Glass>This first story is about somebody asking a piece of technology to do something that, up until recently, only humans have done-- to say something about loss. Something people experience, but of course, machines do not. So the machine has to talk about feelings it cannot know. Tobin Low explains.
<v Ira Glass>第一个故事是关于有人要求一项技术做一件直到最近才由人类完成的事情——谈论失落。这是人们所经历的事情，但机器当然没有这种经历。所以机器必须谈论它无法感受的感情。托宾·洛（Tobin Low）来解释。

00:10:40.700 --> 00:10:55.330
<v Tobin  Low>The technology in this case is called GPT-3. It's an artificial intelligence program that can write remarkably like a human. And generally, it's pretty convincing. It can handle a wide range of topics, too.
<v Tobin  Low>这种情况下的技术被称为GPT-3。这是一种人工智能程序，写作能力像人类一样出色。总的来说，它非常有说服力。它也能处理广泛的话题。

00:10:55.330 --> 00:11:04.320
<v Tobin  Low>The first time Vauhini Vara read something GPT-3 had written, it was about love. The New York Times had it write one of their Modern Love columns on the subject of dating.
<v Tobin  Low>沃希尼·瓦拉（Vauhini Vara）第一次读到GPT-3写的东西时，是关于爱情的。《纽约时报》让它写了一篇关于约会的“现代爱情”专栏。

00:11:04.320 --> 00:11:21.200
<v Vauhini Vara>And then part of what GPT-3 came up with goes like this. "We went out for dinner. We went out for drinks. We went out for dinner again. We went out for drinks again. We went out for dinner and drinks again." So it's like, I read that and was like, wow. That is a very accurate description of modern dating, I think.
<v Vauhini Vara>然后GPT-3写出的一部分内容是这样的：“我们出去吃晚饭。我们出去喝了酒。我们又出去吃晚饭。我们又出去喝了酒。我们又出去吃了晚饭喝了酒。”所以我读到这里时感觉，哇。我认为这是对现代约会非常准确的描述。

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:24.910
<v Tobin Low>Part of me is like, but that's also not very good writing.
<v Tobin Low>我的一部分感觉是，但这写得也不怎么好啊。

00:11:24.910 --> 00:11:31.170
<v Vauhini Vara>It depends on how you look at it. I mean, I feel like Gertrude Stein could have written that, you know? [LAUGHS]
<v Vauhini Vara>这取决于你怎么看。我是说，我觉得格特鲁德·斯坦（Gertrude Stein）也能写出那个，你知道吗？[笑]

00:11:31.170 --> 00:11:31.850
<v Tobin Low>[LAUGHS]
<v Tobin Low>[笑]

00:11:31.850 --> 00:11:36.510
<v Vauhini Vara>It's got this sort of-- if you thought a human had read it, you would think it was deliberately repetitive.
<v Vauhini Vara>它有这种——如果你以为是人类写的，你会觉得它是刻意重复的。

00:11:36.510 --> 00:11:38.380
<v Tobin Low>Like it was making a choice.
<v Tobin Low>就像它是做出了某种选择。

00:11:38.380 --> 00:11:43.850
<v Vauhini Vara>Yes, and it was trying to evoke the tedium of modern dating.
<v Vauhini Vara>是的，它试图唤起现代约会的枯燥感。

00:11:43.850 --> 00:12:04.060
<v Tobin Low>Vauhini, who's a tech journalist and writer herself, was intrigued. She reached out to the people who created the program, got access to try it out. And at first, she just played around with feeding it lines of fiction that she made up, seeing what stories it would spin for her. It was like a game. And as she saw what it could do, she wondered if it could handle more.
<v Tobin Low>沃希尼本人就是一名科技记者和作家，她很感兴趣。她联系了创建该程序的人，获得了试用权限。起初，她只是玩玩，给它输入她编造的小说台词，看看它能为她编织出什么故事。这就像个游戏。当她看到它的能力时，她想知道它是否能处理更多事情。

00:12:04.060 --> 00:12:23.010
<v Vauhini Vara>Just on a whim, I started thinking about the things that are really difficult to write about, the things that, for me, have been really difficult to write about. And at the top of that list is the fact that my sister died many years ago, and it's not something I'd ever tried to write about.
<v Vauhini Vara>就在一时兴起下，我开始思考那些真正难以书写的事情，对我来说一直很难书写的事情。名单榜首就是我姐姐多年前去世的事实，这不是我曾经试图写过的事情。

00:12:23.010 --> 00:12:45.470
<v Tobin Low>Her sister died in 2001, when they were both still in college. In the years since, Vauhini's written fiction. She's reported on other people's stories, but she left the subject of her sister's death untouched, mostly because she couldn't find a way to talk about the loss, let alone write about it. But it occurred to her, maybe GPT-3 was the secret-- a computer program designed to take what you give it and literally write on your behalf.
<v Tobin Low>她姐姐于2001年去世，当时她们还在上大学。此后，沃希尼写过小说。她报道过别人的故事，但她对姐姐去世这个话题避而不谈，主要是因为她找不到一种方式谈论这种失落，更不用说书写它了。但她突然想到，也许GPT-3就是秘诀——一个旨在获取你所给内容并为你书写的计算机程序。

00:12:45.470 --> 00:12:58.010
<v Vauhini Vara>I've got this tool now. I've got this secret thing that I have access to that they're letting me use. I wonder what would happen if I tried to ask this thing for help writing about my sister.
<v Vauhini Vara>我现在有了这个工具。我有了他们让我使用的秘密武器。我想知道如果我试着让这个东西帮我写写我姐姐，会发生什么。

00:12:58.010 --> 00:13:00.200
<v Tobin Low>How did you imagine it was going to go?
<v Tobin Low>你想象会是什么样的过程？

00:13:00.200 --> 00:13:11.170
<v Vauhini Vara>There was this ideal version of it where I fed it a sentence and then it spit out exactly what was in my mind. It somehow read my mind and wrote the rest of it for me.
<v Vauhini Vara>有一个理想的版本，就是我给它输入一句话，然后它就能吐出我脑海里的想法。它以某种方式读懂了我的心，并为我写下了剩下的部分。

00:13:11.170 --> 00:13:34.420
<v Tobin Low>She knew it probably wasn't as skilled as all that, but she was curious how close it could get to expressing something that felt real, familiar. So one night, Vauhini sat up in bed next to her sleeping husband. She opened her laptop, and she started going back and forth with GPT-3. Eventually, she ended up with nine different versions of an essay about her sister.
<v Tobin Low>她知道它可能没那么厉害，但她很好奇它能多接近表达出那种真实、熟悉的感觉。所以一天晚上，沃希尼躺在熟睡的丈夫身边的床上。她打开笔记本电脑，开始与GPT-3反复交流。最终，她得到了九个关于她姐姐的不同版本的文章。

00:13:34.420 --> 00:13:50.310
<v Tobin Low>She started by giving it very little to work with. And then, with each subsequent draft, she fed it more and more. And each time, the AI responded with something different. Some versions are very nuanced. Others, a total miss. Here's version one. Vauhini writes--
<v Tobin Low>她最初只给它很少的内容来处理。然后，随着每一次随后的草稿，她喂给它的内容越来越多。每一次，人工智能都给出了不同的回应。有些版本非常细微。有些则完全跑偏了。这是第一个版本。沃希尼写道——

00:13:50.310 --> 00:13:56.930
<v Vauhini Vara>My sister was diagnosed with Ewing sarcoma when I was in my freshman year of high school and she was in her junior year.
<v Vauhini Vara>我姐姐在我高一、她高三的时候被诊断出患有尤文肉瘤。

00:13:56.930 --> 00:14:06.650
<v Tobin  Low>And in response, GPT-3 picks up the story where she left off. It oddly starts by making up a story about her being an athlete before it talks about the illness.
<v Tobin  Low>作为回应，GPT-3在之后接着编故事。它很奇怪地先编造了一个她成为运动员的故事，然后才谈到疾病。

00:14:06.650 --> 00:14:24.150
<v GPT-3>I had just started playing lacrosse, and she was really excited for that. I don't think I really understood what Ewing sarcoma was. I just knew it was a type of cancer. When I went to visit her in the hospital, it was really the first time I was able to see what this disease was and how it affects the body.
<v GPT-3>我刚开始打长曲棍球，她为此感到非常兴奋。我想我并不真正理解尤文肉瘤是什么。我只知道它是一种癌症。当我去看望医院里的她时，那真的是我第一次看到这种疾病是什么样子以及它是如何影响身体的。

00:14:24.150 --> 00:14:30.510
<v Tobin  Low>GPT-3 talks a bit more about visiting the hospital, but eventually finds its way back to talking about sports.
<v Tobin  Low>GPT-3谈论了更多关于探望医院的事情，但最终还是回到了谈论运动上。

00:14:30.510 --> 00:14:47.420
<v GPT-3>I was able to visit her a lot, but there were times where I couldn't, because we were on the road playing lacrosse. Eventually, she went into remission and got the all-clear and was able to play lacrosse with me for a season. I was a freshman and she was a senior, so that was really cool when she got to play again. She had a lot of friends--
<v GPT-3>我能够经常去看她，但有时候我不能，因为我们在路上打长曲棍球。最终，她进入了缓解期，得到了检查结果一切正常的确认，并能和我一起打了一个赛季的长曲棍球。我是新生，她是高年级学生，所以当她能再次打球时，那真的很酷。她有很多朋友——

00:14:47.420 --> 00:14:59.330
<v Vauhini Vara>The thing about lacrosse was very inaccurate. That's super inaccurate. And then it just goes off the rails when she goes into remission and we play lacrosse together.
<v Vauhini Vara>关于长曲棍球的事情非常不准确。那超级不准确。然后当她进入缓解期我们一起打长曲棍球时，它就彻底脱轨了。

00:14:59.330 --> 00:15:06.090
<v GPT-3>I think that was really special for her because she had a lot of support from people she didn't even know. She's doing great now.
<v GPT-3>我想那对她来说非常特别，因为她得到了许多她甚至不认识的人的支持。她现在做得很好。

00:15:06.090 --> 00:15:15.120
<v Vauhini  Vara>And then that line at the end, "She's doing great now," was really kind of a gut punch because, of course, in reality, my sister died.
<v Vauhini  Vara>然后结尾那句“她现在做得很好”，真的是一种打击，因为当然，在现实中，我姐姐去世了。

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:43.760
<v Tobin Low>Just to explain how GPT-3 goes about making these stories, the software has been fed a huge amount of writing by its creators-- Wikipedia articles, blog posts, Reddit forums, novels-- all examples of how humans write. From that, it's learned what words tend to appear together, sentences and sentiments that follow after each other. And that's what it uses to write its own original sentences and paragraphs, by learning the patterns we make.
<v Tobin Low>为了解释GPT-3是如何编织这些故事的，软件的创建者给它喂入了海量的文字——维基百科文章、博客帖子、Reddit论坛、小说——所有这些都是人类写作的例子。从中，它学会了哪些词往往一起出现，哪些句子和情绪会相互衔接。而这就是它用来编写自己原创句子和段落的方法，通过学习我们创造的模式。

00:15:43.760 --> 00:16:01.980
<v Tobin Low>Sometimes that's as simple as how a sentence is constructed, but it could be as complicated as how we connect ideas. There's a chance, for example, that somewhere in the writing it was fed-- someone wrote about a family member who got cancer and also played lacrosse. In version two, Vauhini starts the same way.
<v Tobin Low>有时候这就像句子结构怎么构成那样简单，但它可能像我们如何连接思想一样复杂。例如，有种可能，在它喂入的某个文本中，有人写过一个家庭成员患了癌症同时也打了长曲棍球。在第二个版本中，沃希尼以同样的方式开始。

00:16:01.980 --> 00:16:08.220
<v Vauhini Vara>My sister was diagnosed with Ewing sarcoma when I was in my freshman year of high school and she was in her junior year.
<v Vauhini Vara>我姐姐在我高一、她高三的时候被诊断出患有尤文肉瘤。

00:16:08.220 --> 00:16:11.100
<v Tobin Low>But this time, she adds the most important fact.
<v Tobin Low>但这次，她加上了最重要的事实。

00:16:11.100 --> 00:16:21.540
<v Vauhiri Vara>I didn't understand then how serious a disease it was, but it was serious. She died four years later. I thought I would die, too, of grief, but I did not.
<v Vauhiri Vara>我当时并不理解这是一种多么严重的疾病，但它确实很严重。四年后她去世了。我以为我会因悲伤而死，但我没有。

00:16:21.540 --> 00:16:33.650
<v Tobin  Low>It's like she's saying, hey, she's not here anymore. Talk about that. GPT-3 doesn't take the bait. Instead, it spits out something that reads like an inspirational article from a fitness magazine.
<v Tobin  Low>就像她在说，嘿，她不在了。谈谈这个吧。GPT-3没上钩。相反，它吐出了一些读起来像是健身杂志上励志文章的内容。

00:16:33.650 --> 00:16:54.440
<v GPT-3>I started a running streak when I was a high school senior to raise money for the Children's Cancer Society. I've run every day since. I've run through rain, heat, snow, and ice. I've run on my best. I've run when I had a cold and when I was sick to my stomach. I've run in the dark. I've run in the daylight, in the moonlight, in the darkness.
<v GPT-3>我高三时开始了一个跑步计划，为儿童癌症协会筹集资金。从那以后我每天都跑。我跑过雨中、酷热、雪中和冰雪中。我在状态最好的时候跑过。我在感冒、胃部不适时跑过。我在黑暗中跑过。我在日光下、月光下、黑暗中跑过。

00:16:54.440 --> 00:17:05.400
<v Tobin Low>You know what? I'm just going to jump in here and say this is kind of a funny bit of writing the machine is doing. It's listing all these things about running with pain, with joy.
<v Tobin Low>你知道吗？我只想插嘴说，这机器写的文字真是有趣。它列出了所有在痛苦中、快乐中跑步的事情。

00:17:05.400 --> 00:17:06.090
<v Vauhini Vara>[LAUGHS]
<v Vauhini Vara>[笑]

00:17:06.090 --> 00:17:12.360
<v GPT-3>I've run when I was sad, when I was happy, when I was angry, when I was afraid.
<v GPT-3>我在伤心、快乐、愤怒、恐惧时都跑过。

00:17:12.360 --> 00:17:14.840
<v Tobin Low>Can I also just ask, are you even a runner?
<v Tobin Low>我也想问一下，你甚至是个跑步者吗？

00:17:14.840 --> 00:17:17.290
<v Vauhini Vara>Never run a day in my life. [LAUGHS]
<v Vauhini Vara>我这辈子从没跑过一天。[笑]

00:17:17.290 --> 00:17:17.940
<v Tobin Low>[LAUGHS]
<v Tobin Low>[笑]

00:17:17.940 --> 00:17:31.020
<v Tobin  Low>I'm going to skip ahead a bit-- I promise you're not missing much more running talk-- and go to the end, where GPT-3 writes a little scene that stars Vauhini at the end of a run. Strangely, it's a meet-cute.
<v Tobin  Low>我要跳过一点——我保证你不会错过更多关于跑步的谈话——直接跳到结尾，那里GPT-3写了一个以沃希尼为主角的小场景。奇怪的是，这是一个邂逅。

00:17:31.020 --> 00:17:51.910
<v GPT-3>I was just wiping the sweat off my face when I looked up and there was a guy standing there. He was a big guy, at least 250 pounds, 6'4" or so. He had a beard and was wearing a tank top. He looked like an ex-Marine. He must have been in his late 20s. He had a six pack of beer in his hand. He said, "Boy, you don't look like much."
<v GPT-3>我正擦着脸上的汗，抬头一看，有个家伙站在那里。他是个大块头，至少250磅，大约6英尺4英寸高。他留着胡子，穿着背心。他看起来像个退伍海军陆战队队员。他一定有20多岁了。他手里拿着一打啤酒。他说：“伙计，你看起来不怎么样。”

00:17:51.910 --> 00:18:08.610
<v Tobin Low>It's at this point Vauhini says that she realized if she really wanted the AI to examine her grief, she was going to have to give it more to work with, more substance.
<v Tobin Low>就在这一点上，沃希尼说她意识到，如果她真的想让AI审视她的悲伤，她必须给它更多东西，更多的实质内容。

00:18:08.610 --> 00:18:17.400
<v Tobin Low>Version three. Again, she writes about her sister being diagnosed freshman year of high school, that she died four years later. And then this time, she adds--
<v Tobin Low>第三个版本。再次，她写了关于她姐姐在高一被诊断，四年后去世。然后这次，她加上了——

00:18:17.400 --> 00:18:27.720
<v Vauhini Vara>I spent the summer at home in Seattle, then returned to college at Stanford. When I arrived there, the campus hadn't changed, but I had. I felt like a ghost.
<v Vauhini Vara>我整个夏天都在西雅图的家里，然后回到斯坦福大学读大学。当我到达那里时，校园没变，但我变了。我觉得自己像个幽灵。

00:18:27.720 --> 00:18:32.630
<v Tobin Low>And here, for a moment, GPT-3 seems to sit with her in that feeling.
<v Tobin Low>在这里，片刻间，GPT-3似乎与她一起沉浸在那种感觉中。

00:18:32.630 --> 00:18:42.830
<v GPT-3>I couldn't speak. I couldn't sleep. I felt my body had died without telling me. I was practicing, though. I was practicing my grief.
<v GPT-3>我无法说话。我无法入睡。我觉得我的身体已经死去了，却没告诉我。不过，我在练习。我在练习我的悲伤。

00:18:42.830 --> 00:18:49.520
<v Tobin  Low>Like, in response, it seems like this is the first time the AI really tries to deal with your grief.
<v Tobin  Low>就像，作为回应，看起来这是AI第一次真正试图处理你的悲伤。

00:18:49.520 --> 00:19:06.060
<v Vauhini Vara>Yeah. I mean, the idea of, like, "I thought my body had died without telling me," "I was practicing my grief," like, those sort of more unusual articulations did really start to ring true to me.
<v Vauhini Vara>是的。我是说，像“我觉得我的身体已经死去了，却没告诉我”，“我在练习我的悲伤”，那种比较不寻常的表达方式真的开始让我觉得真实了。

00:19:06.060 --> 00:19:15.630
<v Tobin Low>Unfortunately, this breakthrough, it's short-lived. GPT-3 again introduces another love interest, a creative writing professor.
<v Tobin Low>不幸的是，这个突破是短暂的。GPT-3再次引入了另一个爱情对象，一位创意写作教授。

00:19:15.630 --> 00:19:35.540
<v GPT-3>One day in the library, I was sitting on a bench crying when a stranger sat down next to me. He said, "I didn't know anything about your sister, but I feel like I know you. I'm in love with you." I said, "I'm sorry." He said, "I think you are, too." He gave me his card.
<v GPT-3>有一天在图书馆，我坐在长椅上哭，一个陌生人坐到我旁边。他说：“我对你姐姐一无所知，但我感觉我认识你。我爱上你了。”我说：“对不起。”他说：“我想你也是。”他给了我他的名片。

00:19:35.540 --> 00:19:40.690
<v Tobin Low>Did it annoy you at all that it was going towards happy resolutions in a couple of these versions?
<v Tobin Low>它在其中几个版本中走向快乐的结局，这让你感到恼火吗？

00:19:40.690 --> 00:20:03.650
<v Vauhini   Vara>It didn't annoy me. I felt a little like we were having a friendly duel or something, me and the AI. I wanted it to express something about me, you know? And it had its own mysterious quasi-consciousness that it was expressing on the page.
<v Vauhini   Vara>它没让我恼火。我觉得我和AI之间有点像在进行一场友好的决斗。我想让它表达一些关于我的东西，你知道吗？它有自己那种神秘的准意识在页面上表达出来。

00:20:03.650 --> 00:20:29.390
<v Tobin Low>I think of that scene from 2001-- A Space Odyssey, when the spaceship' computer, HAL 9000, has already taken on a life of its own, locking a crew member outside of the ship. And I picture Vauhini sitting in an escape pod, asking to be let inside as the computer responds, "I'm sorry. I'm afraid I can't do that."
<v Tobin Low>我想起电影《2001太空漫游》中的那个场景，当宇宙飞船的电脑“哈尔9000”（HAL 9000）已经有了自己的生命，把一名船员锁在飞船外面。我脑补出沃希尼坐在逃生舱里，请求进入，而电脑回应说：“对不起。恐怕我不能那样做。”

00:20:29.390 --> 00:20:44.280
<v Tobin Low>But it's version four where this entire exchange evolves. GPT-3 starts breaking out of cliché, and I think it's because Vauhini gives it something more potent to work with, just one additional sentence, but it feels like a confession of a secret. Here's the end of Vauhini's prompt.
<v Tobin Low>但第四个版本中，整个交流发生了进化。GPT-3开始跳出陈词滥调，我认为这是因为沃希尼给它提供了更强有力的内容，仅仅多了一个句子，但感觉就像坦白了一个秘密。这是沃希尼提示语的结尾。

