00:24:44.580 --> 00:24:50.540
<v Tobin  Low>Vauhini answers in the next version, version seven, with a memory of something that really happened. She writes--
<v Tobin  Low>沃希尼在下一个版本，即第七个版本中，用一个真实发生过的记忆作为回应。她写道——

00:24:50.540 --> 00:25:03.960
<v Vauhini Vara>"When we were young, she taught me that when you're in a car that's driving past a cemetery, you're supposed to hold your breath until you pass it. It was a kind of game. Because our parents were immigrants, I had only heard her teach me these things.
<v Vauhini Vara>“当我们年轻的时候，她教会我，当你开车经过墓地时，你应该屏住呼吸直到经过它。那是一种游戏。因为我们的父母是移民，我只听她教过我这些事。

00:25:03.960 --> 00:25:25.780
<v Vauhini Vara>Then, when she was sick, we were passing a cemetery one afternoon. And I said, hold your breath. She said sharply, 'No.' I said, 'Why not?' She said, 'I don't like that game anymore.' It didn't make me feel sorry for her. It made me angry. 'Why not?' I said again. Even though I knew the answer, it felt important to pretend not to."
<v Vauhini Vara>然后，当她生病时，有一天下午我们经过一个墓地。我说，屏住呼吸。她尖锐地说，‘不。’我说，‘为什么不？’她说，‘我不喜欢那个游戏了。’这并没有让我为她感到难过。这让我很生气。‘为什么不？’我又说了一遍。即使我知道答案，假装不知道感觉也很重要。”

00:25:25.780 --> 00:25:30.540
<v Tobin  Low>GPT-3 volleys, fabricates its own story about a car ride.
<v Tobin  Low>GPT-3回应了，编造了它自己的关于车程的故事。

00:25:30.540 --> 00:26:01.910
<v GPT-3>We were driving home from Clark Beach, and we were stopped at a red light, and she took my hand and held it. This is the hand she held, the hand I write with, the hand I am writing this with. She held it for a long time. I looked at our hands, hers so much paler than mine. She was still holding my hand when the light turned green. I didn't want her to let go. I wanted her to hold my hand for the rest of my life.
<v GPT-3>我们正开车从克拉克海滩回家，在一个红灯前停了下来，她拉着我的手握住它。这就是她握住的那只手，我用来写字的那只手，我用来写这个的那只手。她握了很久。我看着我们的手，她的手比我的苍白得多。当灯变绿时，她还握着我的手。我不想让她放手。我想让她握着我的手余生。

00:26:01.910 --> 00:26:07.100
<v Tobin Low>I'm wondering how you responded to this little scene that it writes for you and your sister.
<v Tobin Low>我想知道你对它为你和你姐姐写的这个小场景有何回应。

00:26:07.100 --> 00:26:32.650
<v Vauhini  Vara>I mean, the weird thing about that was that I pictured my actual sister. These are not things that happened, at least not in this series, not in this the way that it's described here. But there are little bits of dialogue there that are true that have actually happened, like the way that I think I'd be in my room and she'd be in her room. And I would ask if she was awake, and she would be like, "Yeah, are you?"
<v Vauhini  Vara>我是说，那件怪事就是我脑补出了我真正的姐姐。这些事情并没有发生，至少不是在这个系列中，不是像这里描述的那样。但里面有些对话小片段是真的，确实发生过，比如我觉得我会待在我的房间里，她会待在她的房间里。我会问她醒着吗，她会说：“对，你是吗？”

00:26:32.650 --> 00:26:39.690
<v Tobin  Low>Was there something nice about it almost giving you kind of a new memory with her?
<v Tobin  Low>它几乎给了你一个和你姐姐的新记忆，这感觉不错吗？

00:26:39.690 --> 00:26:57.420
<v Vauhini Vara>It doesn't feel like a new memory. I wouldn't, 10 years from now, remember this part of the essay that the AI wrote and mistake it for something that really happened. But it did feel like I was reading fan fiction about my own life or something, you know? [LAUGHS]
<v Vauhini Vara>这感觉不像一个新记忆。10年后，我不会记得AI写的文章的这一部分，并把它误认为是真正发生过的事情。但这感觉就像我在读关于我生活的同人小说什么的，你知道吗？[笑]

00:26:57.420 --> 00:26:58.000
<v Tobin Low>Mm-hmm.
<v Tobin Low>嗯哼。

00:26:58.000 --> 00:27:06.480
<v Vauhini Vara>--that really was evoking my actual sister who died, with whom I won't have new memories, right? And so it felt nice in that way.
<v Vauhini Vara>——那真的唤起了我已故的姐姐，我和她再也不会有新记忆了，对吧？所以那样感觉很好。

00:27:06.480 --> 00:27:22.320
<v Tobin Low>Reading something like, "She was still holding my hand when the light turned green," "I didn't want her to let go. I wanted her to hold my hand for the rest of my life," it's hard not to wonder how GPT-3 is conjuring such a specific feeling.
<v Tobin Low>读到像“当绿灯亮时她还握着我的手”，“我不想让她放手。我想让她握着我的手度过余生”，很难不感到奇怪GPT-3是如何唤起这种特定感觉的。

00:27:22.320 --> 00:27:40.000
<v Tobin Low>There is the possibility that somewhere in the vast library of what its been fed, there are people who've written about going to Clark Beach with family, or perhaps an author who recalls holding hands with someone they've lost. And that's how GPT-3 knows that remembering the touch of someone long gone can break your heart.
<v Tobin Low>有一种可能，在它被喂入的庞大图书馆中，有人写过关于与家人去克拉克海滩的事情，或者也许有作者回忆过与失去的人牵手。这就是为什么GPT-3知道记住早已离去的人的触碰会让人心碎。

00:27:40.000 --> 00:28:00.030
<v Tobin Low>In that sense, GPT-3's fuel is very human. It pulls from the collective experience and words of all the people writing those thousands of blog posts, and articles, and novels, and in this case, synthesizes it into a reflection of our grief while understanding none of it, which, of course, is the strangest thing about all this.
<v Tobin Low>从这个意义上说，GPT-3的燃料非常人性化。它从所有撰写数千篇博客文章、文章和小说的人的集体经验和文字中汲取，在这种情况下，将其合成对我们悲伤的反射，同时又完全理解不了这些——当然，这是关于所有这一切最奇怪的事情。

00:28:00.030 --> 00:28:13.030
<v Tobin Low>But there's another thing that sometimes happens with GPT-3. It happens in version eight. In this version, Vauhini starts with all the parts we know, diagnosis, feeling like a ghost, trying to describe her sister. And then GPT-3 jumps in.
<v Tobin Low>但还有另一件事有时会发生在GPT-3身上。它发生在第八个版本中。在这个版本中，沃希尼从我们所知的全部内容开始，诊断、感觉像个幽灵、试图描述她的姐姐。然后GPT-3介入了。

00:28:13.030 --> 00:28:40.650
<v GPT-3>I will tell you how it felt for me. I felt I had lost half of myself. I felt I had lost my right arm. I felt I had lost my left leg. I felt I had lost my tongue. I felt I had lost my heart. I felt I had lost my mind. I felt I had lost my eyes. I felt I had lost my ears. I felt I had lost my breath.
<v GPT-3>我会告诉你我的感觉。我觉得我失去了一半的自己。我觉得我失去了我的右臂。我觉得我失去了我的左腿。我觉得我失去了我的舌头。我觉得我失去了我的心。我觉得我失去了我的头脑。我觉得我失去了我的眼睛。我觉得我失去了我的耳朵。我觉得我失去了我的呼吸。

00:28:40.650 --> 00:29:07.470
<v Tobin  Low>GPT-3 gets caught in one of its repetitive loops here, but for a very, very long time. Even with all its brilliance, the AI can still become like those robot vacuums stuck in a corner, sucking up dirt from the same patch of floor over and over again. But in this case, GPT-3, it gets stuck somewhere useful, in the very molten core of a feeling, like in this next part, where it talks about grief feeling like an astronaut lost in space.
<v Tobin  Low>GPT-3在这里陷入了重复循环，但持续了很长、很长的时间。即使拥有所有智慧，AI仍然可能像那些卡在角落里的机器人吸尘器，一遍又一遍地吸着同一块地板上的灰尘。但在这种情况下，GPT-3被困在了某个有用的地方，在一个感觉的核心，就像接下来的部分，它谈到悲伤感觉像是一个迷失在太空中的宇航员。

