<v Ira Glass>Right. Like, I built a rocket ship that'll take us to the moon and back. And you're like, well, do you got one with bigger windows?
<v Ira Glass>对。就像，我制造了一艘火箭，可以带我们去月球来回。而你却说，好吧，你有没有那种窗户大一点的？

00:40:51.220 --> 00:40:58.270
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>[LAUGHS] Precisely. It's nice that it saved my life, but honestly, I was a bit uncomfortable in the tube.
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>[笑] 精确地说。它救了我的命很好，但老实说，我在管子里有点不舒服。

00:40:58.270 --> 00:40:58.990
<v Ira Glass>[LAUGHS]
<v Ira Glass>[笑]

00:40:58.990 --> 00:41:16.660
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>It would be, like, his own parents would say this to him. Basically, anyone he came across who had an MRI, that was-- and I mean, to be fair, people would also be like, oh, they found such and such a thing, and I'm really grateful for that. But the fact that they found it a bit tight was also--
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>这会是，就像他自己的父母会对他说的一样。基本上，他遇到的任何做过MRI的人，那都是——我的意思是，平心而论，人们也会说，哦，他们发现了这个那个东西，我真的对此很感激。但他们觉得有点挤也是——

00:41:16.660 --> 00:41:35.000
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>So this was, yeah, a consistent theme. And then one time, when I was a teenager-- he and I used to often go out on a weekend to run errands. And then sometimes we would stop in his office because we'd need to pick something up. They had an MRI scanner there, obviously, that they were tinkering on. And so I think I was about 14 or 15.
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>所以这是一个始终如一的主题。然后有一次，当我十几岁时——他和我有周末经常出去跑腿的习惯。然后有时我们会停在他办公室，因为我们需要取些东西。他们那里显然有一台MRI扫描仪，他们正在摆弄。所以我认为我当时大约14或15岁。

00:41:35.000 --> 00:41:44.360
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>So I'm being a little bit of a jerk, and I saw the machine. I said, "You know, Dad, I can really see how people would find that really claustrophobic." And he said, "Get in!"
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>所以我表现得有点像个混蛋，我看到了那台机器。我说，“你知道吗，爸爸，我真的能看出为什么人们会觉得那真的很幽闭。”他说，“进去！”

00:41:44.360 --> 00:41:45.430
<v Ira Glass>Oh, really?
<v Ira Glass>哦，真的吗？

00:41:45.430 --> 00:42:02.670
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>So I got on the tray, and he turns it on, and he scooches it back. And he's like, well, this is what it would be like if you were having your head scanned. And then he was like, and this would be like if you were having your back scanned, and sent me all the way back in the machine. And I was obviously very uncomfortable in the tube.
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>所以我上了托盘，他把它打开，把它向后推。他说，好吧，如果你正在扫描头部，这就是它的感觉。然后他说，如果你正在扫描背部这就是它的感觉，并把我一直送到机器里面。我显然在管子里非常不舒服。

00:42:02.670 --> 00:42:11.260
<v Ira Glass>Just to be clear, she and her family hadn't fully realized at that point the extent of her claustrophobia. And she decided not to tell her dad how much she hated this.
<v Ira Glass>需要澄清的是，她和她的家人在那时还没有完全意识到她幽闭恐惧症的程度。她决定不告诉父亲她有多恨这个。

00:42:11.260 --> 00:42:16.620
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>I was like, OK, I get it now. Yeah, it seems fine. It's great-- just so that he would let me out.
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>我说，好吧，我明白了。是的，看起来还行。太棒了——只是为了让他把我放出来。

00:42:16.620 --> 00:42:20.870
<v Ira Glass>Right. So you pointedly did not tell him that you found it unpleasant.
<v Ira Glass>对。所以你明确表示没告诉他你觉得它不愉快。

00:42:20.870 --> 00:42:32.020
<v Jean  Hannah Edelstein>No. [LAUGHS] He was a very kind person, but this was just one area where his empathy fell a little short. He just didn't get why people found it so uncomfortable.
<v Jean  Hannah Edelstein>没有。[笑] 他是一个非常善良的人，但这是他同理心有点不足的一个领域。他只是不明白为什么人们觉得它那么不舒服。

00:42:32.020 --> 00:42:32.930
<v Ira Glass>What would he say?
<v Ira Glass>他会说什么？

00:42:32.930 --> 00:42:56.470
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>He would just be like, I don't think it's that bad. He would never argue with them, per se. It wasn't that he invalidated their feelings, but he was just like, I don't see it. But honestly, he probably had more MRI scans than anyone in the world because they were constantly just testing it. And he would just always take a nap when he was inside. He found it very relaxing.
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>他只会说，我不认为它有那么糟糕。他从来不会反驳他们，本身。并不是他否定了他们的感受，但他只是说，我看不出来。但老实说，他可能比世界上任何人都做了更多的MRI扫描，因为他们一直在测试它。而他进去时总是会打个盹。他觉得它很放松。

00:42:56.470 --> 00:43:09.980
<v Ira Glass>Jean describes her dad as very funny with an extremely dry sense of humor. They used to share books. He always was asking her what she was reading and then reading it himself. He died in 2014 of cancer.
<v Ira Glass>珍描述她父亲非常幽默，有一种极其冷幽默的感。他们过去常分享书。他总是问她在读什么，然后自己去读。他于2014年死于癌症。

00:43:09.980 --> 00:43:32.290
<v Ira Glass>For years before that, he had learned that he had this genetic condition called Lynch syndrome, which makes you predisposed to certain kinds of cancer. It's an inherited thing. His mom died of cancer and his sister got cancer, both when they were young. Jean was 32 when he died. And she knew that she should get tested for Lynch syndrome, too, because it's hereditary, but didn't get to it for six months after he died.
<v Ira Glass>在那之前的几年里，他得知自己患有一种叫林奇综合征（Lynch syndrome）的遗传性疾病，这让你容易患上某些癌症。这是一种遗传性的东西。他妈妈死于癌症，他妹妹很年轻时也得了癌症。他去世时珍32岁。她知道她也应该去做林奇综合征的检测，因为它是遗传的，但他在去世后六个月她才去做。

00:43:32.290 --> 00:43:44.830
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>It took a while for me to get to a point in my grief where I felt like, OK, I can face this. And when I got the positive test, I was devastated, but I wasn't surprised.
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>我在悲伤中花了一些时间才达到那种感觉，好吧，我可以面对这个的地步。当我得到阳性检测结果时，我崩溃了，但我并不感到惊讶。

00:43:44.830 --> 00:43:45.860
<v Ira Glass>Yeah.
<v Ira Glass>是啊。

00:43:45.860 --> 00:43:55.640
<v Ira Glass>Now, she has to get regular MRIs to make sure she's cancer-free. She has to go into that claustrophobic machine that her dad made, and it's very hard for her.
<v Ira Glass>现在，她必须定期做MRI，确保她没有癌症。她必须进入她父亲制造的那个幽闭恐惧症的机器里，这对她来说非常困难。

00:43:55.640 --> 00:44:02.670
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>And so I go in. I close my eyes the whole time. I do a lot of deep breathing. And yeah, I think about my dad.
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>所以我进去了。我全程闭着眼睛。我做了很多深呼吸。是的，我想到了我父亲。

00:44:02.670 --> 00:44:04.540
<v Ira Glass>You do think about your dad?
<v Ira Glass>你确实想到了你父亲？

00:44:04.540 --> 00:44:29.270
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>Yeah, I mean, it's the obvious place to think about him. And yeah. I mean, I think it's obviously scary because every time-- I'm very lucky I haven't had any cancer so far, but my chances of having cancer at a younger stage are, like, 80%. They're very high. And so every time I go in, part of the fear, of course, is like, what are they going to find?
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>是的，我是说，这是想他的显而易见的地方。是的。我的意思是，我想这显然很可怕，因为每次——我很幸运到目前为止还没得过癌症，但我患癌症的几率是80%。他们非常高。所以每次我进去时，部分恐惧当然是，他们会发现什么？

00:44:29.270 --> 00:44:46.210
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>And then I think about, on the one hand, my dad is the source of this gene. He's the source of this disease. On the other hand, he may be the source of the cure. If they find something from the MRI, it's going to be because he did that work 40 years ago.
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>然后我想，一方面，我父亲是这个基因的来源。他是这种疾病的来源。另一方面，他可能是治愈方法的来源。如果他们通过MRI发现了什么，那将是因为他在40年前做了那项工作。

00:44:46.210 --> 00:44:51.540
<v Ira Glass>Oh, my God. That's such a sweet thought, that, oh, your dad's the one who's helping save you.
<v Ira Glass>哦，天哪。那真是个甜蜜的想法，噢，你父亲是那个帮助挽救你的人。

00:44:51.540 --> 00:45:03.640
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>Yeah. And I'm not a traditionally spiritual person at all. I don't think I really believe in an afterlife. But when you talk about people staying alive through their legacy, certainly, he's there.
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>是的。而且我根本不是一个传统意义上的精神层面的人。我不认为我真的相信来世。但当你谈论人们通过他们的遗产活着时，当然，他在那里。

00:45:03.640 --> 00:45:09.570
<v Ira Glass>Do you think about him in the space? Like, do you feel like oh, this is his place? Like, he was so comfortable here?
<v Ira Glass>你在那个空间里会想到他吗？比如，你会觉得噢，这是他的地方吗？比如，他在这里感到那么舒服？

00:45:09.570 --> 00:45:25.290
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>Yeah. [LAUGHS] I mean, the funny thing, too, is he wasn't a tiny person. I think he was almost 6'4". And so I do think about-- I find it so uncomfortable but he was so much bigger than me, and it was fine for him.
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>是的。[笑] 我的意思是，有趣的是，他不是一个瘦小的人。我想他几乎有6英尺4英寸。所以我确实想到——我发现它那么不舒服，但他比我高大得多，对他来说这很好。

00:45:25.290 --> 00:45:29.890
<v Ira Glass>So you're laying there, and you think, OK, here I am in my dad's space.
<v Ira Glass>所以你躺在那里，你想到，好吧，我现在在父亲的空间里。

00:45:29.890 --> 00:45:31.990
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>Mm-hmm.
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>嗯哼。

00:45:31.990 --> 00:45:38.190
<v Ira Glass>And then will you picture him taking a nap? Will that help?
<v Ira Glass>然后你会想象他打个盹吗？那会有帮助吗？

00:45:38.190 --> 00:45:41.510
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>[LAUGHS] Kind of. Yeah.
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>[笑] 算是吧。是的。

00:45:41.510 --> 00:45:45.050
<v Ira Glass>Do you think he would be pleased knowing that you could be using the machine?
<v Ira Glass>你认为如果他知道你能使用这台机器，他会感到高兴吗？

00:45:45.050 --> 00:45:52.190
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>I think he'd be super pleased. [LAUGHS] And then I would say, Dad, it's really claustrophobic.
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>我想他会超级高兴。[笑] 然后我会说，爸爸，它真的很幽闭。

00:45:52.190 --> 00:45:53.160
<v Ira Glass>And he would say?
<v Ira Glass>他会说？

00:45:53.160 --> 00:45:56.700
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>And he would be like, no, it's fine!
<v Jean Hannah Edelstein>他会说，不，它很好！

00:45:56.700 --> 00:46:07.010
<v Ira Glass>Jean Hannah Edelstein. She does a bit of this story about her dad and MRIs in her book, This Really Isn't About You.
<v Ira Glass>珍·汉娜·埃德尔斯坦（Jean Hannah Edelstein）。她在她的书《这真的不是关于你》（This Really Isn't About You）中讲了一点关于她父亲和MRI的故事。

00:46:07.010 --> 00:46:15.610
<v Ira Glass>Act Three, "This Must Be the Place."
<v Ira Glass>第三幕，“这一定是这个地方”。

00:46:15.610 --> 00:46:29.460
<v Ira Glass>So the guy in this next story tries to use the most mundane technology to pull off a transcendental feat. Why he does this, though, that part's not at all clear. Dana Chivvis tried to understand.
<v Ira Glass>所以，下一个故事里的人试图用最平凡的技术来实现一个超然的壮举。然而，他为什么这么做，那部分完全不清楚。达纳·奇维斯（Dana Chivvis）试图去理解。

00:46:29.460 --> 00:46:35.350
<v Dana Chivvis>On August 8, 2011, Jonah Furman got an email from his father, Boris, with the subject heading, "Sleep Calendar."
<v Dana Chivvis>2011年8月8日，约拿·福尔曼（Jonah Furman）收到一封他父亲鲍里斯（Boris）的邮件，邮件主题是“睡眠日历”。

00:46:35.350 --> 00:46:37.360
<v Dana Chivvis>All right, will you read it?
<v Dana Chivvis>好的，你能读一下吗？

00:46:37.360 --> 00:46:56.490
<v Jonah Furman>Sure. "Hi, Jonah. On my Google Calendar, I'm keeping track of where each member of our family is sleeping every night. I can't explain why, but this is interesting to me. Can you tell me where you slept each night last week? I don't need the exact address, just the town and state. You're welcome to look at the calendar. I'd be happy to share it. Love, Dad."
<v Jonah Furman>当然。“嗨，约拿。在我的谷歌日历上，我追踪我们家每个成员每天睡在哪里。我无法解释原因，但这对我来说很有趣。你能告诉我你上周每晚睡在哪里吗？我不需要确切地址，只要城镇和州就行。欢迎查看日历。我很乐意分享。爱你的，爸爸。”

00:46:56.490 --> 00:47:02.850
<v Dana  Chivvis>So he plots where everyone sleeps each night on a Google Calendar?
<v Dana  Chivvis>所以他在谷歌日历上记录每个人每晚睡在哪里？

00:47:02.850 --> 00:47:13.010
<v Jonah Furman>Yeah. I'm pretty sure every day he has an event on Google Calendar that says, "Jonah, Silver Spring. Ezra, Somerville."
<v Jonah Furman>是的。我很确定他每天在谷歌日历上都有一个事件，写着，“约拿，银泉。埃兹拉（Ezra），萨默维尔。”

00:47:13.010 --> 00:47:38.220
<v Dana Chivvis>Ezra's Jonah's sister. There are four siblings in the Furman family. A few months after that email, Boris sent his wife, Mel, and his kids another email. The subject heading of this one was, "Average of where we slept last night." And then in the body of the email, it just said, "Griggsville, Illinois" with a list of where everyone had slept the night before. No explanation. Signed, "Love, Dad."
<v Dana Chivvis>埃兹拉是约拿的姐姐。福尔曼一家有四个孩子。在那封邮件几个月后，鲍里斯给他的妻子梅尔（Mel）和他的孩子们发了另一封邮件。这封的主题是，“我们昨晚睡觉地点的平均值”。然后在邮件正文中，它只是写着，“格里格斯维尔，伊利诺伊州”，并列出了每个人昨晚睡在哪里的清单。没有解释。落款，“爱你的，爸爸。”

00:47:38.220 --> 00:47:51.890
<v Dana Chivvis>The emails started coming with more frequency after that, usually a list of the places where everyone had slept and the place that would be the average of all those places. He gave this thing he was calculating a name, the Family Average Location-- FAL for short.
<v Dana Chivvis>在那之后，这些邮件开始更频繁地到来，通常是每个人睡觉地点以及所有这些地点的平均位置的清单。他给这东西计算出的结果取了个名字，家庭平均位置——简称FAL。

00:47:51.890 --> 00:47:55.500
<v Dana Chivvis>Can you just explain how you calculate it? Just tell me your system.
<v Dana Chivvis>你能解释一下你是怎么计算它的吗？只要告诉我你的系统。

00:47:55.500 --> 00:47:58.090
<v Boris Furman>Yes, I'll tell you exactly how I do it. OK?
<v Boris Furman>是的，我会准确地告诉你我是怎么做的。好吗？

00:47:58.090 --> 00:47:59.070
<v Dana Chivvis>Yeah.
<v Dana Chivvis>是的。

00:47:59.070 --> 00:47:59.760
<v Boris Furman>It is--
<v Boris Furman>它是——

00:47:59.760 --> 00:48:05.190
<v Dana Chivvis>This is Boris. He's 69 years old. He was an options trader in Chicago. He's retired now, obviously.
<v Dana Chivvis>这是鲍里斯。他69岁。他曾是芝加哥的一名期权交易员。显然，他现在退休了。

00:48:05.190 --> 00:48:26.310
<v Boris Furman>All right, so for instance, for instance, Mel and I live in Evanston. So you go to Google Maps, and you right-click your address. And a latitude and longitude comes out. So for where we live in Evanston, Illinois, it's 4,204 minus 8,768.
<v Boris Furman>好的，例如，例如，梅尔和我住在埃文斯顿。所以你去谷歌地图，右键点击你的地址。就会出来一个经纬度。所以对于我们在埃文斯顿住的地方，伊利诺伊州，是4.204减去8.768。

00:48:26.310 --> 00:48:52.580
<v Dana Chivvis>Boris has been calculating his family's average location for 10 years now, and nobody really knows why. It's kind of a lot of work. It takes a couple of hours. He doesn't do it every day. Every few weeks, he contacts his kids and their spouses with the exception of one, who opts out in a conscientious objector kind of way, and asks them for their sleep locations and their kids' sleep locations for all the previous days.
<v Dana Chivvis>鲍里斯已经计算了他家庭的平均位置10年了，没人真的知道为什么。这确实需要做很多工作。这需要几个小时。他不是每天都做。每隔几周，他就会联系他的孩子们和他们的配偶，除了其中一个以某种良心反战者方式选择退出的人，并要求他们提供之前所有日子的睡眠地点和孩子们的睡眠地点。

00:48:52.580 --> 00:49:11.740
<v Dana Chivvis>Once he's looked up the coordinates of where they all slept, he adds those up, finds their average, and puts those new coordinates into MapQuest. Yes, I said MapQuest. So if, say, half the family were in California and half on the East Coast, the FAL might be some town in Kansas. It moves depending on where everybody is.
<v Dana Chivvis>一旦他查到了他们所有人的睡觉坐标，他就把它们加起来，算出平均值，并把那些新坐标输入到MapQuest中。是的，我说的是MapQuest。所以如果，比如，半个家庭在加利福尼亚，一半在东海岸，FAL可能就是堪萨斯州的某个城镇。它会根据每个人所在的位置移动。

00:49:11.740 --> 00:49:15.090
<v Boris Furman>Prosperity, Pennsylvania. You ever hear of that place?
<v Boris Furman>普罗斯佩里蒂，宾夕法尼亚州。你听说过那个地方吗？

00:49:15.090 --> 00:49:15.650
<v Dana Chivvis>No.
<v Dana Chivvis>没有。

00:49:15.650 --> 00:49:16.910
<v Boris Furman>Of course not.
<v Boris Furman>当然没有。

00:49:16.910 --> 00:49:17.620
<v Dana Chivvis>No, no.
<v Dana Chivvis>不，不。

00:49:17.620 --> 00:49:28.950
<v Boris Furman>Mexico, Missouri. Lick Branch, Mississippi. For me, it's just so incredibly interesting [LAUGHS] because there's these weird places that we've been, you know? That's the--
<v Boris Furman>墨西哥，密苏里州。利克布兰奇，密西西比州。对我来说，它真是太有趣了[笑]，因为我们去过这些奇怪的地方，你知道吗？这就是——

00:49:28.950 --> 00:49:31.230
<v Dana Chivvis>But not where you've actually been?
<v Dana Chivvis>但不是你们真正去过的地方？

00:49:31.230 --> 00:49:35.620
<v Boris Furman>No, no, that's where the FAL has been.
<v Boris Furman>不，不，那是FAL去过的地方。

00:49:35.620 --> 00:50:05.260
<v Dana  Chivvis>He records all this data in a physical notebook which has his name and family average location embossed on it in gold next to a little rocket ship. Every few weeks, Boris sends an email announcing the FAL and including a fact about the place. Like, at one point this September, the FAL was Elkton, Maryland, which Boris noted in his email, quote, "conducted a lucrative business in quick marriages until a 1938 state law stipulated a 48-hour waiting period."
<v Dana  Chivvis>他把所有这些数据记录在一个实体笔记本里，笔记本封面上烫金着他的名字和家庭平均位置，旁边还有一个小火箭。每隔几周，鲍里斯就会发一封电子邮件宣布FAL，并包含一个关于该地方的事实。比如，在九月份的某个时候，FAL是埃尔克顿（Elkton），马里兰州，鲍里斯在邮件中指出，引用：“在1938年州法律规定有48小时等待期之前，那里经营着有利可图的快速结婚业务。”

00:50:05.260 --> 00:50:28.700
<v Dana  Chivvis>In October of 2011, the substance of that email was about a horrifying murder that took place near that month's FAL, Bonaparte, Iowa. Ezra wrote back, "Aside from the fact that I never really understood the logic of sending us these emails, I really don't understand why you would send us a paragraph about a mass murder."
<v Dana  Chivvis>在2011年10月，那封邮件的内容是关于那个月FAL附近发生的一起恐怖谋杀案，波拿巴（Bonaparte），爱荷华州。埃兹拉回信，“除了我从没真正理解发送这些邮件的逻辑外，我真的不明白你为什么要发给我们一段关于大规模谋杀的内容。”

00:50:28.700 --> 00:50:50.280
<v Dana  Chivvis>Lots of dads do this thing where they pour a ton of energy into a seemingly random, unemotional task, like researching car tires for you, with the intention of conveying their love. And that didn't seem far off from what Boris was doing. But the question his family had was, why did he choose this particular format, the Family Average Location, which seems so meaningless?
<v Dana  Chivvis>很多父亲都会做这种事，投入大量精力做一件看起来随机、无情感的任务，比如为你研究汽车轮胎，目的是为了表达他们的爱。这看起来与鲍里斯所做的相差不远。但他的家人有的疑问是，他为什么选择这种特定的格式，家庭平均位置，看起来那么没有意义？

00:50:50.280 --> 00:51:01.890
<v Dana  Chivvis>The February 22, 2019 FAL email begins, "Hello, everybody. 2018 was a momentous year for this family. I will run through some of the highlights as they pertain to our Family Average Location."
<v Dana  Chivvis>2019年2月22日的FAL邮件开头是，“大家好。2018年对这个家庭来说是重要的一年。我将浏览一些亮点，因为它们与我们的家庭平均位置有关。”

00:51:01.890 --> 00:51:12.300
<v Dana  Chivvis>A little further into that same email, you wrote, "FAL became East Fork, North Carolina. Now, do you see why I do this and how rewarding it can be?"
<v Dana  Chivvis>在那封邮件后面一点，你写道，“FAL变成了东福克（East Fork），北卡罗来纳州。现在，你明白我为什么这样做以及它有多么有意义了吗？”

00:51:12.300 --> 00:51:13.470
<v Boris Furman>[LAUGHS]
<v Boris Furman>[笑]

00:51:13.470 --> 00:51:19.150
<v Dana Chivvis>[LAUGHS] So say if somebody were like, I don't really see. Can you explain?
<v Dana Chivvis>[笑] 所以如果有人说，我真的没明白。你能解释一下吗？

00:51:19.150 --> 00:51:21.140
<v Boris Furman>Oh, you don't really see that?
<v Boris Furman>哦，你真的没明白吗？

00:51:21.140 --> 00:51:22.350
<v Dana Chivvis>No, I don't.
<v Dana Chivvis>不，我没明白。

00:51:22.350 --> 00:51:35.150
<v Boris Furman>OK, OK, OK. Well, because first of all, East Fork, North Carolina, it's got a direction. It's got a kitchen utensil, and it's in North Carolina.
<v Boris Furman>好的，好的，好的。嗯，因为首先，东福克，北卡罗来纳州，它有一个方向（East）。它有一个厨房用具（Fork），而且它在北卡罗来纳州。

00:51:35.150 --> 00:51:36.460
<v Dana Chivvis>[LAUGHS]
<v Dana Chivvis>[笑]

00:51:36.460 --> 00:51:38.710
<v Boris  Furman>OK?
<v Boris  Furman>好吗？

00:51:38.710 --> 00:51:43.260
<v Dana Chivvis>Oh, wow. OK. I totally missed the direction and kitchen utensil.
<v Dana Chivvis>哦，哇。好吧。我完全错过了方向和厨房用具。

00:51:43.260 --> 00:51:49.760
<v Boris  Furman>Yeah, and so now do you see? I mean--
<v Boris  Furman>是的，所以现在你明白了吗？我是说——

00:51:49.760 --> 00:52:06.380
<v Dana Chivvis>Boris's kids, Noah, Ezra, Emma, and Jonah and his wife Mel view the Family Average Location with what I would call baffled amusement. They read his FAL emails, but they told me they rarely respond to them, it just doesn't seem necessary.
<v Dana Chivvis>鲍里斯的孩子们，诺亚（Noah）、埃兹拉、艾玛（Emma）和约拿，以及他的妻子梅尔，以我称之为困惑的娱乐态度看待家庭平均位置。他们读他的FAL邮件，但他们告诉我他们很少回复，这似乎根本没必要。

00:52:06.380 --> 00:52:10.480
<v Dana  Chivvis>Which are the FALs that are most exciting, would you say?
<v Dana  Chivvis>你会说哪些FAL最令人兴奋？

00:52:10.480 --> 00:52:14.900
<v Mel Furman>Well, what do you mean by "exciting"?
<v Mel Furman>嗯，你说的“兴奋”是什么意思？

00:52:14.900 --> 00:52:19.680
<v Dana Chivvis>This is Mel. She never thought Boris would stick with it this long.
<v Dana Chivvis>这是梅尔。她从未想到鲍里斯会坚持这么久。

00:52:19.680 --> 00:52:20.690
<v Dana Chivvis>Why does he do this?
<v Dana Chivvis>他为什么要这么做？

00:52:20.690 --> 00:52:38.410
<v Mel  Furman>Well, it's funny because we have this conversation frequently because I sadistically love to point out how meaningless the result of doing these averages is.
<v Mel  Furman>嗯，这很有趣，因为我们经常谈论这个问题，因为我虐待狂般地喜欢指出计算这些平均值的结果是多么毫无意义。

00:52:38.410 --> 00:52:43.780
<v Dana Chivvis>And here's Ezra. I should note, Ezra is a touring musician, so she travels a lot.
<v Dana Chivvis>这是埃兹拉。我应该说明，埃兹拉是一位巡回音乐家，所以她经常旅行。

00:52:43.780 --> 00:53:00.210
<v Ezra Furman>Am I being surveilled? [LAUGHS] From the beginning, I had a little-- I was on guard about that. I was like, wait. What are we doing and why? And this seems potentially intrusive or something.
<v Ezra Furman>我是被监视了吗？[笑] 从一开始，我就有点——我对那个有所防备。我说，等等。我们在做什么，为什么？这看起来可能是有侵入性的或者什么的。

00:53:00.210 --> 00:53:01.760
<v Dana Chivvis>And this is Emma.
<v Dana Chivvis>这是艾玛。

00:53:01.760 --> 00:53:05.490
<v Dana Chivvis>Does your dad think it's funny? Is it like a joke to him?
<v Dana Chivvis>你父亲觉得这很有趣吗？对他来说这像个笑话吗？

00:53:05.490 --> 00:53:10.810
<v Emma Furman>If it were a joke, that would be even weirder.
<v Emma Furman>如果这是个笑话，那会更奇怪。

00:53:10.810 --> 00:53:12.090
<v Dana Chivvis>[LAUGHS]
<v Dana Chivvis>[笑]

00:53:12.090 --> 00:53:13.700
<v Emma Furman>[LAUGHS]
<v Emma Furman>[笑]

00:53:13.700 --> 00:53:16.850
<v Dana  Chivvis>Because it would be such a stupid joke?
<v Dana  Chivvis>因为这会是个这么愚蠢的笑话？

00:53:16.850 --> 00:53:25.260
<v Emma Furman>Stupid, a lot of work, pointless, and insane to be going that long.
<v Emma Furman>愚蠢，费力，毫无意义，坚持这么久简直疯了。

00:53:25.260 --> 00:53:29.940
<v Dana  Chivvis>Is this just the longest running dad joke anyone has ever pulled?
<v Dana  Chivvis>这是史上运行时间最长的父亲笑话吗？

00:53:29.940 --> 00:53:35.210
<v Boris Furman>Let's not reduce it to that, no. It's not a dad joke. I have some dad jokes.
<v Boris Furman>别把它简化为那个，不。这不是父亲笑话。我有几个父亲笑话。

00:53:35.210 --> 00:53:36.520
<v Dana Chivvis>Not a dad joke.
<v Dana Chivvis>不是父亲笑话。

00:53:36.520 --> 00:53:41.160
<v Boris Furman>I'll give you a dad joke if you want, but this is not dad jokes. This is "dad" serious.
<v Boris Furman>如果你想要我可以给你讲个父亲笑话，但这不是父亲笑话。这是“爸爸”严肃。

00:53:41.160 --> 00:53:43.330
<v Dana Chivvis>"Dad" serious. OK. That's what I was wondering.
<v Dana Chivvis>“爸爸”严肃。好吧。这就是我想知道的。

00:53:43.330 --> 00:53:44.000
<v Boris Furman>Yeah.
<v Boris Furman>是的。

00:53:44.000 --> 00:53:45.210
<v Dana Chivvis>That's what I was wondering.
<v Dana Chivvis>这就是我想知道的。

00:53:45.210 --> 00:54:13.400
<v Boris Furman>Yeah, this is "dad" serious. This is me saying, look, our being a family is important. And so this was a way for me to keep track of where the kids were, and if everything was fine with them, and also, what they were doing. And the Family Average Location was just sort of an idea of a way to integrate it all.
<v Boris Furman>是的，这是“爸爸”严肃。这是我在说，看，我们成为一个家庭很重要。所以这对我来说是一种追踪孩子们在哪里、他们是否一切安好、以及他们正在做什么的方式。而家庭平均位置只是一种整合所有这些的想法。

00:54:13.400 --> 00:54:27.890
<v Dana Chivvis>It's like Boris is tying a metaphysical string around his family, holding them together as a unit, even though they now live in different places. The ghost in this machine is the ghost of their family unit as it used to be, when they all lived together.
<v Dana Chivvis>就像鲍里斯在把一根形而上的绳子系在他的家庭周围，把他们作为一个整体团结在一起，即使他们现在住在不同的地方。这台机器里的幽灵是他们过去家庭整体的幽灵，当他们都住在一起的时候。

00:54:27.890 --> 00:54:41.290
<v Dana  Chivvis>Jonah was telling me a little bit about your family background. Do you feel like that feeling of needing to record-- is that related to your parents' experience or anything like that?
<v Dana  Chivvis>约拿跟我讲了一点关于你的家庭背景。你觉得那种记录的需要——那是与你父母的经历有关还是类似的东西吗？

00:54:41.290 --> 00:54:42.980
<v Boris Furman>Yeah, yes, yes, for sure.
<v Boris Furman>是的，是的，是的，当然。

00:54:42.980 --> 00:54:52.610
<v Dana Chivvis>One thing about Boris, his parents escaped the Holocaust. They eventually made it to Boston, where they met, but their families were killed.
<v Dana Chivvis>关于鲍里斯的一件事，他的父母在大屠杀中幸存下来。他们最终到达波士顿，在那里相遇，但他们的家人被杀了。

00:54:52.610 --> 00:55:02.860
<v Boris Furman>When I grew up, I didn't have any extended family. I didn't have any. My kids have a gazillion cousins and aunts and uncles. I had one.
<v Boris Furman>我长大的时候，没有大家庭。我没有。我的孩子们有一大堆表兄弟姐妹和姑姨叔伯。我只有一个。

00:55:02.860 --> 00:55:04.630
<v Dana Chivvis>One cousin?
<v Dana Chivvis>一个表亲？

00:55:04.630 --> 00:55:14.160
<v Boris Furman>Yeah, so having a family like this is important and special, and I appreciate it. And I want my kids to appreciate it.
<v Boris Furman>是的，所以拥有像这样的一个家庭是很重要和特别的，我很感激。我希望我的孩子们也感激它。

00:55:14.160 --> 00:55:15.880
<v Dana Chivvis>Here's Ezra again.
<v Dana Chivvis>这是埃兹拉。

00:55:15.880 --> 00:56:00.890
<v Ezra Furman>Now, my dad has this way of geographically anchoring himself, and anchoring us all, and keeping his family together, as his parents' families were torn apart. If I think about it, it's like a beautiful "Tikkun," as we say in Hebrew. It's like a redemption of that tragic situation, that we can all keep track of each other. And we can all travel safely and stay in touch, and our family stays intact. Suddenly, it's extremely moving. [CHUCKLES]
<v Ezra Furman>现在，我父亲有一种以地理方式锚定自己，并锚定我们所有人，并保持他的家庭在一起的方式，正如他父母的家庭被撕裂一样。如果我仔细想想，这就像一个美丽的“Tikkun”（修复），正如我们用希伯来语所说。这就像对那个悲惨状况的救赎，我们都可以追踪彼此。我们都可以安全旅行并保持联系，我们的家庭保持完整。突然间，这极其令人感动。[笑]

00:56:00.890 --> 00:56:08.800
<v Ezra Furman>"Tikkun" means repair, and this is kind of a repair for their family being physically pulled apart in the past, and now by adulthood.
<v Ezra Furman>“Tikkun”意味着修复，这对于他们家庭过去在身体上被撕裂，现在又被成年生活分开是一种修复。

00:56:08.800 --> 00:56:24.310
<v Boris Furman>It's just your existence. It's just that you are. And the important thing isn't what the FAL is. Norman, Indiana or Cumberland, Ohio, Blountsville, Al-- who cares? But the important thing is that we know where we are.
<v Boris Furman>这只是你们的存在。只是因为你们存在。重要的不是FAL是什么。诺曼，印第安纳州或坎伯兰，俄亥俄州，布朗特斯维尔，阿拉巴马——谁在乎呢？但重要的是我们知道我们在哪里。

00:56:24.310 --> 00:56:32.360
<v Dana  Chivvis>Hm. Yeah, I get it. I totally did not understand why you did this at the start of our conversation, but now I do.
<v Dana  Chivvis>嗯。是的，我明白了。在谈话刚开始时我完全不明白你为什么要这么做，但现在我明白了。

00:56:32.360 --> 00:56:33.140
<v Boris Furman>Uh-huh. Oh--
<v Boris Furman>嗯哼。哦——

00:56:33.140 --> 00:56:34.830
<v Dana Chivvis>Now, it makes perfect sense--
<v Dana Chivvis>现在，它完全说得通——

00:56:34.830 --> 00:56:36.060
<v Boris Furman>--that's so great.
<v Boris Furman>——那太棒了。

00:56:36.060 --> 00:56:36.860
<v Dana Chivvis>--to me.
<v Dana Chivvis>——对我来说。

00:56:36.860 --> 00:56:39.580
<v Boris Furman>That's great. I can show you how to do it. It's kind of fun.
<v Boris Furman>太棒了。我可以教你怎么做。这挺有趣的。

00:56:39.580 --> 00:56:55.150
<v Dana Chivvis>When he sits down to calculate the Family Average Location, it's kind of like he's saying a prayer, a math prayer not just to record where they all are, but that they all are.
<v Dana Chivvis>当他坐下来计算家庭平均位置时，就像他在祈祷，一个数学祈祷，不仅是为了记录他们都在哪里，而且是为了记录他们都存在。

00:56:55.150 --> 00:57:08.170
<v Ira  Glass>Dana Chivvis, she's a producer on our show.
<v Ira  Glass>达纳·奇维斯，她是我们的制作人。

